@startuml
' ============================================================================
' METADATA
'   author:       TheRobotDrafts Examples
'   description:  Side-by-side comparison of three ERD cardinality notations - IE crow's-foot, Chen/look-across, and simple UML multiplicity labels - on the same Customer-to-Order entity pair
'   usecase:      Educational example showing when each notation style is used and how to read them
'   complexity:   medium
'   domain:       Notation Reference
' ============================================================================
title Example ERD - Crow's-Foot vs IE vs Simple Multiplicity

' ============================================================
' This variant answers a common question: "Why does this
' relationship line look different in tool X vs tool Y?"
' The SAME business rule - "A Customer places zero-or-more
' Orders; each Order belongs to exactly one Customer" - can be
' drawn three ways. All three mean the same thing; the difference
' is notation convention.
' ============================================================
'
' NOTATION 1: IE (INFORMATION ENGINEERING) CROW'S-FOOT
'   Uses symbols at the line endpoints:
'     ||  = exactly one (a bar)
'     o{  = zero-or-many (a circle + crow's foot)
'     |{  = one-or-many (a bar + crow's foot)
'     o|  = zero-or-one (a circle + bar)
'   This is the DEFAULT in most modern ERD tools (dbdiagram.io,
'   Lucidchart, DBeaver, DataGrip). PlantUML uses this natively.
'
' NOTATION 2: CHEN / LOOK-ACROSS
'   Uses cardinality numbers or letters ON the line itself:
'     1, N, M  or  (0,n), (1,1), (0,*)
'   The numbers are placed near the OPPOSITE entity (look-across
'   rule). PlantUML can emulate this with multiplicity labels.
'
' NOTATION 3: SIMPLE UML MULTIPLICITY
'   Uses 1, 0..1, *, 1..* labels in quotes near each end.
'   Common in UML class-diagram-style tools adapted for ERD.
'   PlantUML supports this via "1" -- "*" syntax.
' ============================================================

' ============================================================
' SHARED ENTITIES (identical structure for all three comparisons)
' ============================================================

entity "Customer" as cust_a {
  * customer_id : BIGINT <<PK>>
  --
  email : VARCHAR(255) NN
  name : VARCHAR(100) NN
}

entity "Order" as ord_a {
  * order_id : BIGINT <<PK>>
  --
  customer_id : BIGINT <<FK>> NN
  order_date : TIMESTAMP NN
}

entity "Customer" as cust_b {
  * customer_id : BIGINT <<PK>>
  --
  email : VARCHAR(255) NN
  name : VARCHAR(100) NN
}

entity "Order" as ord_b {
  * order_id : BIGINT <<PK>>
  --
  customer_id : BIGINT <<FK>> NN
  order_date : TIMESTAMP NN
}

entity "Customer" as cust_c {
  * customer_id : BIGINT <<PK>>
  --
  email : VARCHAR(255) NN
  name : VARCHAR(100) NN
}

entity "Order" as ord_c {
  * order_id : BIGINT <<PK>>
  --
  customer_id : BIGINT <<FK>> NN
  order_date : TIMESTAMP NN
}

' ============================================================
' STYLE 1: IE CROW'S-FOOT (PlantUML native)
' ||--o{ = exactly-one on the left, zero-or-many on the right.
' Read: "One Customer has zero-or-many Orders."
' ============================================================
note top of cust_a
  <b>Style 1: IE Crow's-Foot</b>
  The symbol at each endpoint encodes
  cardinality directly.
  || = one      o{ = zero-or-many
  This is the most common style in
  modern database tools.
end note

cust_a ||--o{ ord_a : "places (IE)"

' ============================================================
' STYLE 2: CHEN / LOOK-ACROSS with multiplicity labels
' PlantUML renders "1" and "0..N" as labels near each end.
' In true Chen notation the cardinality is read by looking at
' the FAR end of the line from each entity. Here we attach
' explicit labels to approximate that reading.
' ============================================================
note top of cust_b
  <b>Style 2: Chen / Look-Across</b>
  Cardinality numbers sit ON the line.
  Read by looking across to the opposite
  entity's end. Often uses 1 / N / M or
  (0,n) / (1,1) style labels.
  Common in academic textbooks.
end note

cust_b "1" -- "0..N" ord_b : "places (Chen)"

' ============================================================
' STYLE 3: SIMPLE UML MULTIPLICITY
' Uses the "1" .. "*" label syntax borrowed from UML class
' diagrams. * means "many" (0..*), 1 means "exactly one".
' ============================================================
note top of cust_c
  <b>Style 3: Simple UML Multiplicity</b>
  Minimal labels: 1, 0..1, *, 1..*
  Borrowed from UML class diagrams.
  Lightweight; no endpoint symbols.
  Good for quick whiteboard sketches.
end note

cust_c "1" -- "*" ord_c : "places (UML)"

' ============================================================
' SUMMARY COMPARISON TABLE (as a note)
' ============================================================
note as ComparisonNote
  <b>When to use each notation</b>
  --
  <b>IE Crow's-Foot</b>
  Use in production database design and reverse-engineering
  output. Tools like dbdiagram.io, DBeaver, DataGrip, ER/Studio,
  and PlantUML default to this. Endpoints are unambiguous once
  learned, and they scale to dense diagrams without overlapping
  text.
  --
  <b>Chen / Look-Across</b>
  Use in academic settings, textbooks, and when you need to
  annotate participation/dependency constraints explicitly
  (total vs partial, weak entities). The look-across reading can
  confuse newcomers but maps cleanly to formal set theory.
  --
  <b>Simple UML Multiplicity</b>
  Use for quick communication, whiteboard sessions, and when
  your audience already knows UML class diagrams. Least formal;
  pairs well with application-layer modeling where you mix
  classes and tables in one diagram.
end note

ord_a .. ComparisonNote
ComparisonNote .. ord_b

' ============================================================
' READING CROW'S-FOOT SYMBOLS - QUICK REFERENCE
'   ||--||    one-to-one       (exactly one / exactly one)
'   ||--o|    one-to-zero-or-one
'   ||--o{    one-to-zero-or-many  (most common for FK parents)
'   ||--|{    one-to-one-or-many   (identifying / mandatory)
'   }o--o{    many-to-many         (resolve with a join table)
'
' LEFT SIDE codes (closest to first entity):
'   ||  exactly one
'   o|  zero or one
'   }o  zero or many
'   }|  one or many
'
' RIGHT SIDE codes (closest to second entity) use the same set
' mirrored:  ||  o{  |{  o|
' ============================================================

@enduml
